<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunni–Shia divide</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sunni-Shia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>–Shia divide is the split between the two main branches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which originated in the dispute over the succession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> trace its origins to the succession debate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqifah">Saqifah</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> note how Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a> deepened the divide.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dispute begun by the succession crisis, over whether legitimate leadership belonged to the Prophet’s family or rested on the community’s choice, produced the <b>Sunni-Shia divide</b> that has run through Islamic history ever since.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Safavid-Ottoman rivalry turned the <b>Sunni-Shia divide</b> into a geopolitical fault line, and the repeated wars of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries produced frontiers that followed confession as much as strategy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Sectarian and social differences, the <b>Sunni–Shia divide</b> and the clash between secular states such as Lebanon and Syria and religious Saudi Arabia, raised doubts about whether the project was viable at all.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
